## Exercise

You have an in-memory array of product records. Implement three things in JavaScript:

1. `linearFindBySku(products, sku)` — scan the whole array.
2. `createSkuIndex(products)` — build a lookup structure keyed by `sku`.
3. `indexedFindBySku(index, sku)` — use that structure for fast lookup.

Then extend it so inserts keep the array and index consistent. Finally, benchmark 100,000 products and 1,000 lookups.

This is the same idea as a database index: trade extra memory and write-time work for much faster reads.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Naive linear search

Without an index, every lookup scans from the beginning.

```js
function linearFindBySku(products, sku) {
  return products.find(product => product.sku === sku) ?? null;
}
```

- Time: `O(n)` per lookup.
- Fine for small arrays or rare queries.
- Becomes expensive when called many times.

### Step 2: Build an index with `Map`

A JavaScript `Map` gives average `O(1)` key lookup. Here, the key is `sku`, and the value is the product object.

```js
function createSkuIndex(products) {
  const index = new Map();

  for (const product of products) {
    // Treat SKU as unique. If duplicates exist, decide a policy:
    // first wins, last wins, or store an array of products.
    if (!index.has(product.sku)) {
      index.set(product.sku, product);
    }
  }

  return index;
}
```

This is like:

```sql
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_products_sku ON products(sku);
```

### Step 3: Fast lookup using the index

```js
function indexedFindBySku(index, sku) {
  return index.get(sku) ?? null;
}
```

- Average time: `O(1)`.
- Worst case can be worse than a balanced database B-tree if hash collisions occur.
- A database B-tree index is usually `O(log n)` but supports ordered/range queries better than a hash map.

### Step 4: Keep index and data consistent on writes

The main cost of an index is maintenance. Every insert, update, or delete must update both the table and the index.

```js
function addProduct(products, index, product) {
  if (index.has(product.sku)) {
    throw new Error(`Duplicate SKU: ${product.sku}`);
  }

  products.push(product);
  index.set(product.sku, product);
  return product;
}

function removeProductBySku(products, index, sku) {
  const product = index.get(sku);
  if (!product) return null;

  const position = products.indexOf(product);
  if (position !== -1) products.splice(position, 1);

  index.delete(sku);
  return product;
}
```

### Step 5: Composite keys

Real indexes can include multiple columns. In JavaScript, create a stable composite key.

```js
function compositeKey(category, sku) {
  return `${category}\u0000${sku}`;
}

const compositeIndex = new Map();
compositeIndex.set(compositeKey("books", "SKU-001"), product);
```

Use a separator that cannot appear in your data, or use a nested `Map`.

