## Node.js: reading files

### Asynchronous with promises (recommended)

```js
import { readFile, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";

async function main() {
  try {
    const text = await readFile("./data.txt", "utf8");
    console.log(text);

    await writeFile("./out.txt", "Hello from Node\n", "utf8");
    console.log("Written");
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("File error:", err.code, err.message);
  }
}

main();
```

### Synchronous (simple scripts, CLI tools)

```js
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";

const text = readFileSync("./data.txt", "utf8");
writeFileSync("./out.txt", text.toUpperCase(), "utf8");
```

Use sync APIs carefully in servers; they block the event loop.

### Binary data

```js
import { readFile, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";

const buffer = await readFile("./image.png"); // Buffer (no encoding)
await writeFile("./copy.png", buffer);
```

### Streams for large files

```js
import { createReadStream, createWriteStream } from "node:fs";
import { pipeline } from "node:stream/promises";

await pipeline(
  createReadStream("./huge.log"),
  createWriteStream("./huge.copy.log")
);
```

Streams avoid loading entire files into memory and support transforms (compression, parsing, etc.).

